1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,760
Hello and welcome back to
the Articulate Journal.

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:06,785
In the last episode, I
explained why Articulate

3
00:00:06,825 --> 00:00:09,433
is being designed as an AI-native system

4
00:00:09,473 --> 00:00:12,080
and what I meant by
AI-native architecture.

5
00:00:12,120 --> 00:00:15,427
In this episode, I want
to take a step back

6
00:00:15,467 --> 00:00:18,440
for a moment and look at
something more fundamental.

7
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:22,200
AI-native systems are still systems.

8
00:00:22,240 --> 00:00:25,440
Every production system is ultimately
judged by the same question.

9
00:00:25,480 --> 00:00:27,640
Can people trust it?

10
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:30,680
That's where system
characteristics become important.

11
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:32,382
There's a huge amount of excitement

12
00:00:32,422 --> 00:00:33,800
around AI at the moment.

13
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:36,120
Every week there's a new model, a

14
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:38,440
new framework or another
agent platform promising

15
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:40,760
to change the way we build software.

16
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:43,760
Those advances are genuinely
exciting, but we must not

17
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:46,760
forget the fundamentals of
software engineering because once a

18
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:49,760
system is in production,
no one really cares how

19
00:00:49,800 --> 00:00:52,760
clever it is if they can't rely on it.

20
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:56,893
They care whether it's available,
whether it's secure, whether it

21
00:00:56,933 --> 00:01:01,027
performs well, whether they can
trust the answers it produces,

22
00:01:01,067 --> 00:01:05,160
whether someone can understand what
happened when something goes wrong.

23
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:08,258
In other words, they care about

24
00:01:08,298 --> 00:01:10,840
the characteristics of the system.

25
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:13,200
Features make a system useful.

26
00:01:13,240 --> 00:01:16,120
Characteristics make it trustworthy.

27
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:19,334
Before we start exploring
runtimes, workflows and multi-agent

28
00:01:19,374 --> 00:01:22,548
systems, I think it's worth taking a step

29
00:01:22,588 --> 00:01:25,360
back and asking a much simpler question.

30
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:29,400
What qualities does every
production system need to possess?

31
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:31,516
As architects, we tend to think

32
00:01:31,556 --> 00:01:33,280
about software in two dimensions.

33
00:01:33,320 --> 00:01:36,240
The first is functionality,
as in what does

34
00:01:36,280 --> 00:01:39,200
a system do, and the
second is characteristics.

35
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:41,320
How well does it do it?

36
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:44,440
One thing that's always fascinated
me about architecture is

37
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:47,560
that we rarely choose
patterns for our own sake.

38
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:50,640
We don't choose CQRS
because it's fashionable.

39
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:53,760
We don't choose event-sourcing
because it's interesting.

40
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:56,340
We don't introduce durable workflows,

41
00:01:56,380 --> 00:01:58,920
simply because the technology exists.

42
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:02,562
We choose architecture approaches
because they improve characteristics that

43
00:02:02,602 --> 00:02:05,800
matter for the problem
we're trying to solve.

44
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:09,640
Every architectural decision
is really a trade-off.

45
00:02:09,680 --> 00:02:13,880
Improve one characteristic and
another may become more difficult.

46
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:19,560
Caching improves performance, but
introduces consistency challenges.

47
00:02:19,600 --> 00:02:23,204
Creating a system
improves scalability while

48
00:02:23,244 --> 00:02:26,240
making observability
and reliability harder.

49
00:02:26,280 --> 00:02:29,551
Adding autonomous agents may increase

50
00:02:29,591 --> 00:02:32,200
flexibility, but reduce predictability.

51
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:35,560
Architecture has never been
about finding the perfect design.

52
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:38,960
It's about deciding which
trade-offs are appropriate.

53
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,120
So let's look at some of those
characteristics that matter.

54
00:02:42,160 --> 00:02:45,120
The first is trust.

55
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:48,600
Trust has always been
fundamental to software.

56
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:53,549
Users expect systems to protect
their information, respect

57
00:02:53,589 --> 00:02:57,880
permissions, record important
actions and behave safely.

58
00:02:57,920 --> 00:03:01,600
AI-native systems introduce new questions.

59
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:04,800
Which models are allowed
to access this information?

60
00:03:04,840 --> 00:03:07,720
Which tools can an agent invoke?

61
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:11,440
Can retrieved information
cross security boundaries?

62
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:14,880
Can we explain why a
particular answer was produced?

63
00:03:14,920 --> 00:03:19,680
Can an agent perform an
action without human approval?

64
00:03:19,720 --> 00:03:23,960
Security is no longer about
protecting APIs and databases,

65
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:28,240
it's about protecting the intelligence
operating inside the system.

66
00:03:28,280 --> 00:03:31,035
Being capable of doing
something is not the

67
00:03:31,075 --> 00:03:33,480
same as being authorised to do it.

68
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:35,360
Next comes reliability.

69
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:39,440
Eventually, every production
system experiences failure.

70
00:03:39,480 --> 00:03:43,382
A model provider becomes
unavailable. A retrieval

71
00:03:43,422 --> 00:03:46,760
service times out. A tool fails.

72
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:49,720
A workflow is interrupted.

73
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:53,640
Reliability isn't about pretending
those failures won't happen, it's about

74
00:03:53,680 --> 00:03:57,560
deciding what the system should
do when they inevitably do.

75
00:03:57,600 --> 00:03:58,560
Can we retry?

76
00:03:58,600 --> 00:04:01,640
Can we continue using another model?

77
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:04,240
Can it pause safely and resume later?

78
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:06,280
Can it ask a human for help?

79
00:04:06,320 --> 00:04:08,462
Or can it preserve enough state

80
00:04:08,502 --> 00:04:10,280
to continue without starting again?

81
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:16,040
That's what users
experience as reliability.

82
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:18,840
Performance is another
characteristic that becomes

83
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:21,640
increasingly interesting
in AI native systems.

84
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:24,760
Traditionally, we measured things
like latency and throughput.

85
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:31,800
Those still matter, but AI systems
also introduce perceived performance.

86
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:34,360
Streaming a response
often feels much faster

87
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:36,920
than waiting several seconds
before anything appears.

88
00:04:36,960 --> 00:04:39,914
At the same time, reasoning itself has

89
00:04:39,954 --> 00:04:42,480
become part of the execution path.

90
00:04:42,520 --> 00:04:46,508
Retrieval, calling tools,
validation and model inference

91
00:04:46,548 --> 00:04:49,960
all contribute to the overall experience.

92
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:54,802
Optimizing one stage doesn't
necessarily make the system feel faster,

93
00:04:54,842 --> 00:04:59,160
and performance always needs to
be considered alongside cost.

94
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:02,380
Sometimes a larger model
is worth using, sometimes

95
00:05:02,420 --> 00:05:05,600
a smaller model is more than good enough.

96
00:05:05,640 --> 00:05:07,680
Scalability has also changed.

97
00:05:07,720 --> 00:05:11,120
Cloud native systems taught us
how to scale infrastructure.

98
00:05:11,160 --> 00:05:14,440
AI-native systems
introduce another question.

99
00:05:14,480 --> 00:05:16,920
How do we scale intelligence?

100
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:21,040
One option is to build a single
increasingly capable agent.

101
00:05:21,080 --> 00:05:24,142
Give it more tools, more memory, a

102
00:05:24,182 --> 00:05:26,800
larger context, a more capable model.

103
00:05:26,840 --> 00:05:30,882
At first, this seems attractive
because everything happens in one

104
00:05:30,922 --> 00:05:34,964
place, but eventually the context
grows, the prompt becomes more

105
00:05:35,004 --> 00:05:39,046
complicated, tool selection becomes
harder, and the agent spends more

106
00:05:39,086 --> 00:05:42,720
effort managing its own knowledge
than solving the problem.

107
00:05:42,760 --> 00:05:45,000
The alternative is to scale out.

108
00:05:45,040 --> 00:05:49,560
Instead of one larger agent, we
create several specialized ones.

109
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:52,060
Each has a focused responsibility, a

110
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:54,560
smaller context and a clearer purpose.

111
00:05:54,600 --> 00:05:57,769
That often improves
clarity and isolation, but

112
00:05:57,809 --> 00:06:00,520
it introduces another kind of complexity.

113
00:06:00,560 --> 00:06:02,900
Now the architecture has to decide

114
00:06:02,940 --> 00:06:05,280
which agent should receive the request.

115
00:06:05,320 --> 00:06:09,590
How context is transferred, how
work is coordinated, how failures

116
00:06:09,630 --> 00:06:13,900
are recovered, and how reasoning
is observed across multiple agents.

117
00:06:13,940 --> 00:06:17,879
In other words, scale
out moves complexity from

118
00:06:17,919 --> 00:06:21,360
inside the agent into
the architecture itself.

119
00:06:21,400 --> 00:06:23,840
Neither approach is automatically right.

120
00:06:23,880 --> 00:06:26,940
Just as with microservices,
the goal isn't to create

121
00:06:26,980 --> 00:06:30,040
the biggest component or the
greatest number of components.

122
00:06:30,080 --> 00:06:33,518
The goal is to place complexity where

123
00:06:33,558 --> 00:06:36,500
it can be understood and managed.

124
00:06:36,540 --> 00:06:39,892
That leads to a principle I've started

125
00:06:39,932 --> 00:06:42,800
using throughout Articulate,
coherence before size.

126
00:06:42,840 --> 00:06:45,260
An agent should remain coherent.

127
00:06:45,300 --> 00:06:48,004
Its responsibilities, tools, and contexts

128
00:06:48,044 --> 00:06:50,200
should naturally belong together.

129
00:06:50,240 --> 00:06:53,969
Splitting an agent simply because
smaller agents appear more

130
00:06:54,009 --> 00:06:57,320
sophisticated usually creates
more problems than it solves.

131
00:06:57,360 --> 00:07:00,160
Observability becomes
even more important once

132
00:07:00,200 --> 00:07:03,000
intelligence becomes part of the runtime.

133
00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:05,840
Traditional systems give us
logs, metrics and traces.

134
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:10,397
AI-native systems also need
us to understand prompts,

135
00:07:10,437 --> 00:07:14,953
retrieved knowledge, routing
decisions, tools that have been

136
00:07:14,993 --> 00:07:18,940
called, reasoning paths,
confidence, and evaluation results.

137
00:07:18,980 --> 00:07:21,342
When an answer is wrong, knowing that

138
00:07:21,382 --> 00:07:23,400
one service called another isn't enough.

139
00:07:23,440 --> 00:07:27,600
We also need to understand
why that decision was made.

140
00:07:27,640 --> 00:07:30,500
Another characteristic that becomes

141
00:07:30,540 --> 00:07:33,400
increasingly important is evolvability.

142
00:07:33,440 --> 00:07:34,960
Models will change.

143
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,040
Prompt strategies will change.

144
00:07:37,080 --> 00:07:38,680
Frameworks will change.

145
00:07:38,720 --> 00:07:43,800
The architecture shouldn't depend
on today's implementation choices.

146
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:47,553
Responsibilities should
remain understandable even if

147
00:07:47,593 --> 00:07:50,680
tomorrow's runtime looks
completely different.

148
00:07:50,720 --> 00:07:53,080
Operability matters for the same reason.

149
00:07:53,120 --> 00:07:55,640
Someone has to support these systems.

150
00:07:55,680 --> 00:07:59,640
Operators may need to disable a
model, change routing policies,

151
00:07:59,680 --> 00:08:03,240
inspect a reasoning path, or
replay a failed execution.

152
00:08:03,280 --> 00:08:06,120
An elegant architecture that can't

153
00:08:06,160 --> 00:08:09,000
be operated effectively rarely succeeds.

154
00:08:09,040 --> 00:08:14,146
Finally, there's one characteristic
that I think AI-native systems

155
00:08:14,186 --> 00:08:18,720
have brought much more clearly
into focus, evaluation.

156
00:08:18,760 --> 00:08:22,720
Traditional software engineering
gives us testing: unit

157
00:08:22,760 --> 00:08:26,720
tests, integration tests
and performance tests, etc.

158
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:33,320
Those remain essential, but AI systems
introduce probabilistic behavior.

159
00:08:33,360 --> 00:08:35,960
A workflow can complete successfully and

160
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:38,160
still produce a poor answer.

161
00:08:38,200 --> 00:08:41,560
The software worked, the behavior didn't.

162
00:08:41,600 --> 00:08:47,400
Testing tells us whether the
implementation functions correctly.

163
00:08:47,440 --> 00:08:50,389
Evaluation tells us whether the system

164
00:08:50,429 --> 00:08:52,880
actually achieved its intended outcome.

165
00:08:52,920 --> 00:08:57,467
I think evaluation will become one
of the defining characteristics

166
00:08:57,507 --> 00:09:02,053
for AI-native systems, not because
testing becomes less important, but

167
00:09:02,093 --> 00:09:06,640
because trustworthy behavior becomes
just as important as correct execution.

168
00:09:06,680 --> 00:09:11,000
Ultimately, that's what this
episode is really about.

169
00:09:11,040 --> 00:09:13,860
AI-native systems
introduce new technologies,

170
00:09:13,900 --> 00:09:16,720
new runtimes, and new patterns.

171
00:09:16,760 --> 00:09:20,274
But they don't replace the foundations of

172
00:09:20,314 --> 00:09:23,320
software architecture.
They build upon them.

173
00:09:23,360 --> 00:09:26,400
Remember: AI-native
systems are still systems.

174
00:09:26,440 --> 00:09:30,644
They're judged by their reliability,
their security, their scalability,

175
00:09:30,684 --> 00:09:35,360
their observability, and above
all, whether people can trust them.

176
00:09:35,400 --> 00:09:37,900
Those characteristics
don't become less important

177
00:09:37,940 --> 00:09:40,440
because intelligence has
entered the architecture.

178
00:09:40,480 --> 00:09:42,720
They become even more important.

179
00:09:42,760 --> 00:09:46,443
In the next episode, we'll
build on these ideas by

180
00:09:46,483 --> 00:09:50,166
looking at the architectural
principles that are beginning to shape

181
00:09:50,206 --> 00:09:53,889
Articulate, and why thinking in
terms of capabilities rather than

182
00:09:53,929 --> 00:09:57,280
components provides a stronger foundation
for building AI-native systems.
